      section: "CS",
      q: "Consider an arbitrary set of CPU-bound processes with unequal CPU burst lengths submitted at the same time to a computer system. Which one of the following process scheduling algorithms would minimize the average waiting time in the ready queue?",
      options: ["Shortest remaining time first","Round-robin with time quantum less than the shortest CPU burst","Uniform random","Highest priority first with priority proportional to CPU burst length"],
      answer: 0,
      marks: 1,
      explanation: "Compare the candidates against the known optimality result for scheduling. 1. Since all processes are CPU-bound and arrive simultaneously with unequal, KNOWN burst lengths, the scheduler is free to always pick whichever process has the shortest remaining CPU burst next. 2. It's a standard theorem in scheduling theory that, for any fixed batch of jobs with known execution times, running the SHORTEST job (or shortest remaining time) first minimizes the average waiting time — intuitively, short jobs get out of the way quickly so fewer processes are made to wait behind a long one. 3. Check the alternatives: Round-robin with a tiny quantum (B) forces every process to take turns regardless of length, so long processes repeatedly delay short ones across many rounds, increasing average wait compared to SRTF. Uniform random (C) picks jobs with no regard to length, so on average it performs far worse than always favoring the shortest job. Priority proportional to burst length (D), if interpreted as favoring the LONGEST job first (higher burst = 'higher priority' number), is the exact opposite of SRTF and would maximize, not minimize, average waiting time. 4. Since all processes arrive at the same time, SRTF behaves identically to plain SJF (non-preemptive) here (no preemption opportunity exists since there's nothing arriving later to preempt for), and this shortest-first ordering is exactly what minimizes total (and hence average) waiting time. So option (A), Shortest remaining time first, is correct."
