      explanation: "Check each option against the standard definition of ACID. 1. Atomicity (A): a transaction is 'all or nothing' — either all its operations take effect or none do. This IS one of the four ACID properties. 2. Consistency (B): a transaction must take the database from one valid (consistency-constraint-satisfying) state to another valid state. This IS one of the four ACID properties. 3. Isolation (C): concurrently executing transactions must not see each other's intermediate, uncommitted state — the effect must be as if transactions ran one at a time. This IS one of the four ACID properties. 4. The fourth genuine ACID property, notably absent from the options, is Durability — once a transaction commits, its effects survive even a subsequent crash. 5. 'Deadlock-freedom' (D) is a desirable property of a CONCURRENCY-CONTROL or LOCKING PROTOCOL (e.g., ensuring transactions never wait on each other in a cycle), but it is not part of the ACID acronym at all — a system can satisfy ACID while still being vulnerable to deadlocks (which are typically handled separately via detection/prevention, not guaranteed away by ACID itself). So (D) is the one NOT part of ACID, making it the correct answer."
