      section: "CS",
      q: "Consider a proposed solution for the critical section problem with n processes P0...Pn-1, where pmax returns an integer not smaller than any of its arguments, and t[i] is initialized to zero for all i. Code for Pi: do { c[i]=1; t[i] = pmax(t[0],...,t[n-1])+1; c[i]=0; for every j != i { while (c[j]); while (t[j] != 0 && t[j] <= t[i]); } Critical Section; t[i]=0; Remainder Section; } while (true); Which one of the following is TRUE about the above solution?",
      options: ["At most one process can be in the critical section at any time","The bounded wait condition is satisfied","The progress condition is satisfied","It cannot cause a deadlock"],
      answer: 0,
      marks: 2,
      explanation: "Trace through what the algorithm forces before any process can enter its critical section. 1. When process Pi wants to enter, it sets c[i]=1 (announcing 'I am choosing a ticket'), computes t[i] = pmax(t[0],...,t[n-1]) + 1 (a ticket number strictly greater than every other process's CURRENT ticket, so tickets are effectively totally ordered by (t[i], i) as in a bakery-style algorithm), then sets c[i]=0 (announcing 'I have my ticket'). 2. Before entering, Pi then loops over every OTHER process j: first it waits while c[j] is 1 (waiting for Pj to finish choosing its ticket, so tickets are compared only once both are stable), then it waits while t[j] is nonzero (meaning Pj also wants to enter) AND t[j] ≤ t[i] (meaning Pj's ticket is smaller-or-equal, so Pj has priority and must go first). 3. Because every active ticket t[j] is drawn to be strictly greater than all others seen so far, and ties are effectively impossible to both hold simultaneously past the choosing phase (the smaller-ticket process is always let through first), at most one process can ever have the globally smallest active ticket and pass every one of its waiting checks at the same time — so at most one process is ever inside the critical section. This directly gives mutual exclusion, matching option (A). 4. The bounded-wait and progress properties are actually WHERE this algorithm has issues (relevant to why B, C, D are not the right description): a subtle timing where two processes end up computing t[i] concurrently based on a stale pmax value can, in pathological interleavings, let ticket numbers coincide or extra waiting occur, and this construction as literally written does not cleanly guarantee bounded waiting or deadlock-freedom in the way a fully correct bakery algorithm (with an added ID tie-breaker) would — but what the algorithm DOES robustly guarantee, by construction of the ticket comparison loop, is that two processes can never simultaneously satisfy the entry condition, hence mutual exclusion (A) is the property that is TRUE."
